 \begin{abstract}
-Mixture-of-expert adapters are a natural way to adapt frozen time series foundation models (TSFMs)---yet they \textbf{catastrophically fail} on TSFMs with instance normalization: routing entropy collapses to $0.000{\pm}0.000$ because normalization strips the per-window statistics the router needs. We isolate this \textbf{normalization-induced routing collapse} through seven converging causal controls---most strikingly, opposite entropy trajectories on identical data (Timer-XL rises $1.49{\to}1.60$ while MOMENT collapses $0.60{\to}0.00$)---while all eleven standard MoE-rescue mechanisms fail ($2.7{\times}$ worse; 720 runs). A mutual-information decomposition formalizes the mechanism and yields a signal-ratio predictor that identifies where collapse occurs ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$), including boundary cases where it does \emph{not} (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix is unambiguous: \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)} routes on raw, pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} ($p{<}0.001$) across six backbones (two negative controls confirm normalization-specificity). A predicted consequence---the \textbf{Frozen Paradox}---is confirmed: frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$, because unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse. A self-routed dual-stream extension matches or beats DLinear on \textbf{6/6 datasets} at $H{=}96$.
+Mixture-of-expert adapters are a natural way to adapt frozen time series foundation models (TSFMs)---yet they \textbf{catastrophically fail} on TSFMs with instance normalization: routing entropy collapses to $0.000{\pm}0.000$ because normalization strips the per-window statistics the router needs. We isolate this \textbf{normalization-induced routing collapse} through seven converging causal controls---most strikingly, opposite entropy trajectories on identical data (Timer-XL rises $1.49{\to}1.60$ while MOMENT collapses $0.60{\to}0.00$)---while all eleven standard MoE-rescue mechanisms fail ($2.7{\times}$ worse; 720 runs). A mutual-information decomposition formalizes the mechanism and yields a signal-ratio predictor that identifies where collapse occurs ($\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$), including boundary cases where it does \emph{not} (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). Because the diagnosis pinpoints the cause, the fix is unambiguous: \textbf{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)} routes on raw, pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} ($p{<}0.001$) across six backbones (two negative controls confirm normalization-specificity). A predicted consequence---the \textbf{Frozen Paradox}---is confirmed: frozen adapters beat full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$, because unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse. An extension restoring raw-signal access to experts matches or beats DLinear on 6/6 datasets (Appendix).
 \end{abstract}

 \section{Introduction}
@@ -62,7 +62,7 @@ Mixture-of-expert adapters are a natural way to adapt frozen time series foundat

 \textbf{The problem with one-size-fits-all adapters.} Today, every public TSFM benchmark adapts the backbone with a single static head that pools hidden states and projects them to the forecast horizon~\citep{goswami2024moment, das2024timesfm, nie2023patchtst, hu2022lora}. But real time series are non-stationary---ETTh1 mixes quiet baseline segments with sharp seasonal excursions---and a single adapter topology cannot be optimal for every input window. A natural remedy is a \emph{mixture of expert adapters}: a pool of topologically distinct heads with a learned router that assigns each window to the most suitable expert.

-\textbf{Why keep the backbone frozen?} Frozen-backbone adaptation enables deployment regimes that per-dataset models like DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} cannot serve: multi-tenant cloud serving with hot-swappable adapters, on-device deployment with over-the-air adapter updates, and cross-task reuse (Appendices~\ref{app:deployment},~\ref{app:benchmark}). As TSFMs scale toward billion-parameter regimes~\citep{shi2024timemoe}, frozen-backbone PEFT becomes the primary per-dataset adaptation path.
+\textbf{Why keep the backbone frozen?} Frozen-backbone adaptation enables deployment regimes that per-dataset models like DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} cannot serve. A single TSFM in GPU VRAM serves thousands of tenants via 426K-parameter adapters (${\sim}1.7$\,MB each) swapped in microseconds---eliminating $O(N)$ independent training pipelines. On-device deployment and cross-task reuse impose similar constraints (Appendices~\ref{app:deployment},~\ref{app:benchmark}). As TSFMs scale toward billion-parameter regimes~\citep{shi2024timemoe}, frozen-backbone PEFT becomes the primary per-dataset adaptation path.

 \textbf{The collapse problem.} This natural mixture-of-experts approach \emph{catastrophically fails} on TSFMs that use instance normalization. When the standard MoE recipe~\citep{wang2022adamix, fedus2022switch}---routing on hidden states with load balancing---is applied to MOMENT with RevIN, routing entropy drops to $0.000\pm0.000$ in 7/12 unfrozen configurations (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}). The root cause is \textbf{normalization-induced co-adaptation}: the dominant expert receives larger gradients, which reshape the backbone to serve it even more, creating a feedback loop that collapses within ${\sim}50$ training steps (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}). Even with a frozen backbone, hidden-state routing still underperforms because RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} strips the per-window mean and variance that the router needs to distinguish different input regimes. This is normalization-specific: Moirai~\citep{woo2024moirai}, which uses LayerNorm instead of RevIN, does \emph{not} collapse (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}).

@@ -74,7 +74,7 @@ Our contributions are threefold:

 \textbf{(2)~Fix and a predicted consequence.} Because the principle pinpoints the cause, the fix is unambiguous: RR-MoA routes on raw pre-normalization input, achieving \textbf{54/54 wins} ($p{<}0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}). The principle also predicts a \textbf{Frozen Paradox}: unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}), so frozen adapters should beat full fine-tuning---confirmed at $12$--$79\%$ across all six datasets. RR-MoA generalizes across four RevIN backbones; two negative controls (Chronos, Timer-XL) confirm normalization-specificity.

-\textbf{(3)~Actionable diagnosis: predicting and closing the DLinear gap.} The diagnosis predicts that restoring raw-signal access to the experts should close the remaining gap to supervised baselines. We validate this prediction: SR-RIA\textsuperscript{+}, combining self-routing with a dual-stream architecture, matches or beats DLinear on \textbf{6/6 datasets} at $H{=}96$ (up to $-7.5\%$; \S\ref{sec:main_results}), with multi-backbone generalization achieving $107/123$ cells across six backbones and four horizons.
+\textbf{(3)~Actionable diagnosis.} The diagnosis is further validated by its predictive power: restoring raw-signal access to the experts closes the gap to supervised baselines, matching or beating DLinear on 6/6 datasets at $H{=}96$ ($107/123$ cells across six backbones; \S\ref{sec:main_results}, Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}).

 \section{Related Work}

@@ -340,7 +340,7 @@ In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) a

 \textbf{Self-routing and architecture-agnostic validation.} To confirm the bottleneck is in the routing \emph{input} rather than the router \emph{architecture}, we test two additional controls. First, Self-Routed MoA (SR-MoA)~\citep{lv2025aoe,cheng2025ermoe,liu2026routingfree}: each expert contains its own sigmoid gate---no external router. On raw input, SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (ETTh1: $0.464$; ETTm1: $0.388$; Weather: $0.222$; 252 runs total), generalizes across six backbones, and independently replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6 datasets; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). The same architecture gating on hidden states degrades by $+87\%$ to $+768\%$ on \textbf{all 6 datasets} ($p{=}0.016$, Wilcoxon). \textbf{Normalization-specificity:} on Timer-XL (no RevIN), the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$---$15$--$100{\times}$ smaller than on MOMENT---confirming the degradation is normalization-induced. Second, replacing AdaMix's hidden-state router with a raw-input router recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}). Both confirm the diagnosis: the bottleneck is normalization, not router design. \textbf{Summary of converging controls:} (C1)~RevIN ablation recovers entropy (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}, green rows); (C2)~opposite trajectories on MOMENT vs Timer-XL (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}); (C3)~BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm collapse identically (Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}); (C4)~11 rescue mechanisms fail (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}); (C5)~raw-input AdaMix router recovers (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}); (C6)~SR-MoA raw vs hidden ($+87$--$768\%$; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}); (C7)~cross-modality replication on ViT (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}); (C8)~re-injecting $(\mu,\sigma)$ into hidden states does \emph{not} recover routing ($+42$--$239\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:reinjection}).

-{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the simplicity of the fix is the point.} The contribution is the \emph{diagnosis}, not the fix. Before this work, MoE routing collapse was treated as an optimization problem (load balancing, entropy regularization, z-loss) and the assumption was that better training recipes would resolve it. We show it is an \emph{information} problem: normalization structurally removes the signal the router needs, and no optimization-side trick can compensate (eleven mechanisms, 720 runs; Table~\ref{tab:rescue}). That the fix is unambiguous---route on the signal that was stripped---is evidence that the causal mechanism is correctly identified.}
+{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the simplicity of the fix is the point.} Identifying the correct cause is the hard part; that the fix follows trivially from the diagnosis is itself evidence the causal mechanism was correctly identified. For years the MoE community attacked routing collapse from the optimization side---load balancing, entropy regularization, z-loss---under the assumption that better training recipes would resolve it. We show the problem is informational, not optimization-related: normalization structurally removes the signal the router needs, and no optimization-side trick can compensate (eleven mechanisms, 720 runs; Table~\ref{tab:rescue}).}
