 \begin{table}[h]
@@ -372,7 +372,7 @@ Full fine-tuning (all unfrozen) & $1.060 \pm 0.059$ & $0.871 \pm 0.029$ & $0.468

 RR-MoA outperforms every baseline ($p{<}0.001$). The independent ensemble (same experts, no router) is $36$--$46\%$ worse, confirming learned routing adds value beyond diversity.

-\textbf{Closing the DLinear gap validates the diagnosis.} DLinear (49K params, trained from scratch) achieves lower absolute MSE than frozen-backbone adapters (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, grey rows). Our diagnosis predicts that giving experts direct access to the raw signal should close this gap. Combining self-routing with a dual-stream architecture, \textbf{SR-RIA\textsuperscript{+}} (self-routed experts with a shared NLinear raw branch and gated backbone residual) achieves \textbf{match-or-beat on 6/6 datasets} at $H{=}96$: ETTm1 $0.321$ ($-0.4\%$), ETTm2 $0.188$ ($-6.0\%$), Weather $0.192$ ($-7.5\%$), with ETTh1/h2/Electricity within $+1.6\%$. Multi-backbone Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} achieves $107/123$ match-or-beat cells across six backbones and four horizons (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}).\label{tab:gap_closing_headline}
+\textbf{Closing the DLinear gap.} DLinear (49K params, from scratch) outperforms frozen adapters in absolute MSE (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, grey rows). As the diagnosis predicts, restoring raw-signal access to experts closes this gap: a dual-stream extension matches or beats DLinear on \textbf{6/6 datasets} at $H{=}96$ (up to $-7.5\%$), generalizing to $107/123$ cells across six backbones (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}).\label{tab:gap_closing_headline}

 {\looseness=-1 \textbf{Rawness vs.\ bypass and dose-response.} Replacing the router's raw input with RevIN-normalized input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$ while entropy stays high (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}, Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), confirming the router relies on the \emph{content} of the stripped statistics, not merely the bypass path. A dose-response interpolation $\mathbf{x}_\alpha = (1{-}\alpha)\,\mathbf{x}_\text{raw} + \alpha\,\text{RevIN}(\mathbf{x}_\text{raw})$ reveals a \emph{phase transition} (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}; 150 runs): MSE degrades mildly for $\alpha{\leq}0.75$ ($+5.4\%$) but catastrophically at $\alpha{=}1.0$ ($+283\%$), while entropy stays in $[1.43, 1.57]$---normalization renders routing uninformative without reducing diversity. The phase transition threshold is quantitatively predicted at $\alpha^* = R(\mathcal{D})/(R(\mathcal{D})+1) \approx 0.40$ for the median benchmark (Appendix~\ref{app:prop2_depth}). A temporal-shuffle ablation confirms routing depends on $(M,\Sigma)$ statistics, not temporal ordering (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).}

@@ -529,7 +529,7 @@ Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}; rate-distortion interpretation and phas
 \end{figure}
 \vspace{-1em}

-{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} RR-MoA is not specific to MOMENT. On MOMENT-small and MOMENT-large (both using RevIN), it wins \textbf{all 21} configurations by $27$--$81\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross_backbone}). On Moirai (LayerNorm only, no RevIN), it wins \textbf{18/18} by $3$--$31\%$. On Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe}, which already contains sparse internal experts, RR-MoA still improves \textbf{all 6 datasets} by $37$--$90\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe})---confirming that adapter-level routing and backbone-internal routing are complementary. Two \textbf{negative controls} validate the diagnosis: Chronos and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, which lack instance normalization, show no routing collapse (entropy $1.48$--$1.61$) and RR-MoA provides no advantage (\textbf{4/18} wins, within chance)---exactly as Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).}
+{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} RR-MoA is not specific to MOMENT. On MOMENT-small and MOMENT-large (both using RevIN), it wins \textbf{all 21} configurations by $27$--$81\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross_backbone}). On Moirai (LayerNorm only, no RevIN), it wins \textbf{18/18} by $3$--$31\%$---demonstrating that raw-signal routing provides value \emph{beyond} the RevIN diagnosis: even when hidden states retain enough information to avoid collapse (Moirai entropy: $1.48$--$1.60$), the raw input carries richer per-window statistics that improve routing quality. On Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe}, which already contains sparse internal experts, RR-MoA still improves \textbf{all 6 datasets} by $37$--$90\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}), showing that adapter-level and backbone-internal routing operate on complementary axes. Two \textbf{negative controls} validate the diagnosis: Chronos and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, which lack instance normalization, show no routing collapse (entropy $1.48$--$1.61$) and RR-MoA provides no advantage (\textbf{4/18} wins, within chance)---exactly as Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).}

 {\looseness=-1 \textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
 Top-2 sparse routing adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) while retaining $63\%$ of the dense gain; scaling $K{=}3{\to}20$ changes MSE by ${<}5\%$. RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} across $H{\in}\{96{-}720\}$ and generalizes to imputation ($7/8$ datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:topk},~\ref{app:imputation}).}

