 \textbf{Self-routing and architecture-agnostic validation.} To confirm the bottleneck is in the routing \emph{input} rather than the router \emph{architecture}, we test two additional controls. First, Self-Routed MoA (SR-MoA)~\citep{lv2025aoe,cheng2025ermoe,liu2026routingfree}: each expert contains its own sigmoid gate---no external router. On raw input, SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (ETTh1: $0.464$; ETTm1: $0.388$; Weather: $0.222$; 252 runs total), generalizes across six backbones, and independently replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6 datasets; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). The same architecture gating on hidden states degrades by $+87\%$ to $+768\%$ on \textbf{all 6 datasets} ($p{=}0.016$, Wilcoxon). \textbf{Normalization-specificity:} on Timer-XL (no RevIN), the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$---$15$--$100{\times}$ smaller than on MOMENT---confirming the degradation is normalization-induced. Second, replacing AdaMix's hidden-state router with a raw-input router recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}). Both confirm the diagnosis: the bottleneck is normalization, not router design. \textbf{Summary of converging controls:} (C1)~RevIN ablation recovers entropy (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}, green rows); (C2)~opposite trajectories on MOMENT vs Timer-XL (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}); (C3)~BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm collapse identically (Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}); (C4)~11 rescue mechanisms fail (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}); (C5)~raw-input AdaMix router recovers (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}); (C6)~SR-MoA raw vs hidden ($+87$--$768\%$; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}); (C7)~cross-modality replication on ViT (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}); (C8)~re-injecting $(\mu,\sigma)$ into hidden states does \emph{not} recover routing ($+42$--$239\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:reinjection}).

-{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the simplicity of the fix is the point.} The contribution is the \emph{diagnosis}, not the fix. Before this work, MoE routing collapse was treated as an optimization problem (load balancing, entropy regularization, z-loss) and the assumption was that better training recipes would resolve it. We show it is an \emph{information} problem: normalization structurally removes the signal the router needs, and no optimization-side trick can compensate (eleven mechanisms, 720 runs; Table~\ref{tab:rescue}). That the fix is unambiguous---route on the signal that was stripped---is evidence that the causal mechanism is correctly identified.}
+{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why the simplicity of the fix is the point.} Identifying the correct cause is the hard part; that the fix follows trivially from the diagnosis is itself evidence the causal mechanism was correctly identified. For years the MoE community attacked routing collapse from the optimization side---load balancing, entropy regularization, z-loss---under the assumption that better training recipes would resolve it. We show the problem is informational, not optimization-related: normalization structurally removes the signal the router needs, and no optimization-side trick can compensate (eleven mechanisms, 720 runs; Table~\ref{tab:rescue}).}
